    "lawSection": "Real Property Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2967A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to the definition\nof qualified leasehold condominiums\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo include the New York City educational construction fund (\"Fund\") as\nan additional holder of the landlord's interest in such leasehold\ninterest under the definition of \"Qualified Leasehold Condominium.\"\nThe bill will permit the Fund to create commercial, industrial,\nresidential condominium interests or mixed-use condominium interests\nin leaseholds of real property controlled by the Fund.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 of the bill amends subdivision 12 of section 339-e of the\ncondominium act, which is article 9-B of the Real Property Law (the\n\"Act\"). Section 339-e includes a list of definitions of terms used in\nthe Act. Subdivision 12 is the definition of \"qualified leasehold\ncondominium\". The bill amends this definition to include commercial,\nindustrial or mixed-use including residential condominiums built on\nleasehold interests in real property controlled by the Fund. This\nlegislation is specific to one location in the borough of Manhattan.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides that it shall take effect immediately.\n\n EFFECTS OF PRESENT LAW WHICH THIS BILL WOULD ALTER :\nAdds new paragraph (iv) to subdivision 12 of section 339-e to the Real\nProperty Law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nPursuant to the New York City Educational Construction Fund (\"Fund\"),\na public benefit corporation created by the State legislature in 1966,\nthe City of New York conveyed land to the Fund at no cost for the\ndevelopment of twelve schools. The land for ten schools is being used\nfor combined schools and housing units and the land for two schools is\nbeing used for combined schools and commercial buildings. The\noperating revenues consist of rental revenues and tax equivalency\npayments.\n\nCurrently, the non-school residential portions of the Fund projects\nconsist of leasehold co-ops. The Fund is not able to market these\nnonschool portions as residential leasehold condominiums. The\nperception of condominiums amongst the development community is that\nbuyers of residential units are more likely to purchase condominium\nunits, and are also more willing to pay higher prices for these\ncondominium units, as opposed to co-ops.\n\nAllowing the Fund to market the non-school portions of its projects as\nresidential leasehold condominiums would broaden the appeal of its\nsites and afford greater flexibility to meet changing market\nconditions. This will create a more viable residential program thus\nensuring a successful continuation of the Educational Construction\nFund Program.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008: A.11548/S.8559 Referred to Assembly Housing Committee. Passed\nSenate.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
